## Ownership, capabilities and sequence

Root controller is the communication bridge and read-only advisor. High-profile
coordinator `/root/terminal_progress` owns feature/docs integration and acceptance
coordination. `implement` owns automate.sh, lib/process.sh, new lib/progress.sh,
checks/run.sh and approved production helper additions. `tests` owns the new
test_terminal_progress.py and dedicated fixtures. Writers preserve others' edits.
No specialist delegates. Agent creation by profile and independent context was
observed; four active slots allow root, coordinator and two specialists. Fresh
review and validation contexts follow writers sequentially as slots become free.
No implementer is reused as independent reviewer or validator.

The coordinator owns any D-05 integration in scripts/config/automation.json and
scripts/config/native/bandit/bandit-baseline.json after independent review of the
final test source and observed native occurrences. This narrow disposition does
not change selection, executable identity, argv, severity rules or parser gates.

Review relevant ADRs scripts-shell-refactor, scripts-task-closure and
repository-maintenance, including their dated successors. Existing historical
feature evidence stays historical; new focused observations cannot renew its
hashes or approve native/product results.

## Acceptance wording successor — 2026-11-15

The maintainer requested clarification of “Execução não aceita o produto.”
The two fixed sentences now state that functional acceptance is evaluated
separately. This is a presentation-only successor under REQ-03/D-02; state/code
contracts and criteria are unchanged. The same request reviews history false
positives in the separate history-scanner-correction feature. Current terminal
fixtures and regression checks are reobserved because progress.sh and the history
configuration digest changed. Prior envelopes remain under
docs/validation/history-correction-20261115/coordinator/.


## Terminal styling successor — 2026-11-15

This presentation-only extension uses normal coordination because the selected
rendering surface and focused oracles are known. The original full-route feature
and its independent review/validation gates remain applicable. No scanner policy,
process supervision, selection, publication or raw-stream behavior is changed.

D-06 / R-06 (REQ-03, REQ-05): reuse the shared progress renderer for all scopes.
Centralize trusted phase headings, field indentation and textual status styling;
use normal ANSI SGR 32/31/33 for the requested dark green/red/yellow roles. Terminal
palettes determine the actual RGB values, so no universal contrast or specific
monitor appearance is claimed. Status words remain readable without color, and
recorded measurements retain their distinct semantics. Capture the caller's
NO_COLOR preference before execution-environment defaults are applied, preserving
NO_COLOR=1 for tool processes. Redirected output and TERM=dumb remain plain.
Verify with PTY and plain fixtures, existing outcome/progress/byte/cancellation
regressions, and an observed controlled preview; do not execute a real complete
scanner run for this styling task.

Selected primary method for this successor: `.codex/skills/design-system/SKILL.md`,
bridge `sdd/chain/design-system.md` version 1; entry SHA-256
`bd5c8bac71c275efc4cfc2ae31b2ac7d2e009014a32ffecb91918e5b39279e96`.
Actually consulted `references/pattern-decisions.md` and
`references/accessibility.md`. Contribution: reuse the common terminal presentation,
name colors by state, preserve text alternatives, and distinguish tested terminal
behavior from accessibility conformance. DESIGN.md and docs/design/surfaces.md
were reviewed: native Iced surface/token changes are outside this CLI successor.

Ownership: coordinator `/root/terminal_style` integrates evidence/convergence and
documentation. `implement` owns scripts/lib/progress.sh, the minimal automate.sh
integration and new focused style tests. `docs` owns this spec/plan/tasks,
scripts/README.md and the dated report. Writers preserve all preexisting changes.
After writers finish, new independent review and validation contexts assess the
stabilized inputs; no implementation context supplies its own independent approval.
Prior envelopes and contracts remain under
`docs/validation/terminal-style-20261115/before/`.

## Publication repair successor — 2026-11-15

D-07 / R-07 (REQ-02/REQ-06): the finalization heartbeat must not mutate the run
while publication enumerates/copies/removes it. Diagnose the observed Directory
not empty failure with a deterministic delayed-publication fixture. Prefer a
read-only finalization projection; stopping all finalization feedback is an
alternative with reduced visibility, and retrying deletion would hide the race.
Retain existing deletion/retention policy and emergency diagnostics. Correct
SC2250 expansions and explicitly capture SC2312 producer failures.

Primary method actually consulted for this successor:
`.codex/skills/reliability-engineering/SKILL.md`, bridge
`sdd/chain/reliability-engineering.md` version 1, and resource-lifecycle reference.
Contribution: identify the owned watcher/write lifecycle, contain mutation before
publication, and observe cleanup and failure/cancellation transitions. Input hashes
identify the consulted content revision in the current evidence.

Ownership: `/root/repair` coordinates and owns feature/report/scripts README;
`implement` owns progress.sh and new test_terminal_publication.py; the existing
authenticated test_terminal_progress.py remains unchanged. Existing working
changes are preserved. Profile-based creation, fresh context, message/state and
interrupt tools are available; root+coordinator+two specialists is the active
limit. Implementation precedes fresh independent review and validation; slot
reuse is observed by successful subsequent creation, not assumed. No extra-high
implementation effort is required for this bounded lifecycle repair.
